UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a17B$assigns lipid/steroid synthesis to the RER instead of the SER$a17B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a17C$confuses the Golgi's sorting/tagging role with the synthetic site of the hormone$a17C$ FROM q;

-- Q9 [Golgi Apparatus · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g18)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Golgi Apparatus',
    $qt18$A plasma cell has just finished synthesizing and folding an antibody (immunoglobulin) destined for release into the bloodstream. Before the antibody leaves the cell, one organelle modifies it, sorts it, and packages it into a secretory vesicle that buds off for export. Which organelle carries out this final modification, sorting, and packaging step?$qt18$,
    $op18$[{"label":"A","text":"The rough endoplasmic reticulum"},{"label":"B","text":"The Golgi apparatus"},{"label":"C","text":"A lysosome"},{"label":"D","text":"A mitochondrion"}]$op18$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex18$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the Golgi apparatus is the organelle where finished proteins are modified, sorted, and packaged into secretory vesicles for export from the cell. After the antibody is synthesized and folded, transport vesicles deliver it to the Golgi, where "sorting, tagging, packaging, and distributing lipids and proteins takes place" (lines 5754-5755); the "modified and tagged proteins are packaged into secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi's trans face," and some of these "fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell" (lines 5771-5773). The text specifically notes that "immune system cells that secrete antibodies have an abundance of Golgi" (lines 5776-5777), which is exactly the situation of a plasma cell. (Choice A) The rough ER is where the antibody is synthesized and folded, not where the finished protein is sorted and packaged for export; ribosomes there "transfer their newly synthesized proteins into the RER's lumen where they undergo structural modifications, such as folding," after which the protein leaves via "transport vesicles that bud from the RER's membrane" to reach the Golgi (lines 5699-5705). (Choice B) The Golgi apparatus is correct because it modifies and sorts the antibody and packages it into secretory vesicles that bud from the trans face for release (lines 5754-5755, 5771-5773). (Choice C) A lysosome is the cell's "garbage disposal," using enzymes that "aid in breaking down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids, and even worn-out organelles" (lines 5548-5550); it carries out intracellular degradation, not packaging of a protein for export. (Choice D) A mitochondrion is a "double membrane" organelle whose cristae and matrix "have different roles in cellular respiration" (lines 5485-5488); it produces ATP and has no role in modifying or packaging secreted proteins. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the Golgi apparatus is the organelle that modifies, sorts, and packages finished proteins into secretory vesicles for export.$ex18$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc18$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc18$, 'biology', 1, 45
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a18A$Confusing the site of protein synthesis/folding (rough ER) with the site of final sorting and packaging for export (Golgi)$a18A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a18C$Confusing an organelle of intracellular degradation (lysosome) with an organelle of secretory packaging$a18C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a18D$Naming an unrelated organelle (mitochondrion) whose true role is energy production, not protein export$a18D$ FROM q;

-- Q10 [Golgi Apparatus · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g19)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Golgi Apparatus',
    $qt19$A researcher fuses GFP to a transmembrane protein and watches it bud from the endoplasmic reticulum, enter a Golgi stack, transit the cisternae, and finally leave the Golgi inside a vesicle headed for the plasma membrane. Which assignment of the entry and exit faces most directly fits this itinerary?$qt19$,
    $op19$[{"label":"A","text":"The protein enters at the trans face and exits from the cis face."},{"label":"B","text":"The protein both enters and exits at the cis face, the side nearest the ER."},{"label":"C","text":"The protein enters at the cis face and exits from the trans face."},{"label":"D","text":"The protein enters at the trans face and exits from a lateral cisterna midway along the stack."}]$op19$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex19$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because ER-derived transport vesicles fuse with the cis face (the side nearest the ER) for entry, and finished proteins exit packaged in vesicles that bud from the trans face. The Golgi apparatus is polarized: the textbook states that \"The side of the Golgi apparatus that is closer to the ER is called the cis face. The opposite side is the trans face. The transport vesicles that formed from the ER travel to the cis face, fuse with it, and empty their contents into the Golgi apparatus' lumen\" (line 5764-5766). Cargo then transits the cisternae picking up modifications, and \"the modified and tagged proteins are packaged into secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi's trans face\" (line 5771). Because a GFP-tagged transmembrane protein arriving from the ER and departing toward the plasma membrane must trace ER \(\rightarrow\) cis \(\rightarrow\) trans \(\rightarrow\) destination, entry at the cis face and exit at the trans face is the only directionally consistent assignment. (Choice A) This reverses the polarity: it places ER-derived entry at the trans face and budding at the cis face, but the cis face—not the trans face—is the one \"closer to the ER\" that receives ER vesicles (line 5764-5765), so the direction is backward. (Choice B) Entry at the cis face is correct, but exit also at the cis face contradicts the text, which states finished proteins \"bud from the Golgi's trans face\" (line 5771); a single face cannot serve as both the ER-receiving and the destination-budding side in a polarized stack. (Choice C) Correct: entry at the cis face matches \"transport vesicles that formed from the ER travel to the cis face, fuse with it\" (line 5765-5766), and exit at the trans face matches secretory vesicles that \"bud from the Golgi's trans face\" (line 5771); the cis-to-trans flow is the defining polarity of the organelle. (Choice D) Entry at the trans face is again the reversed direction, and budding from a midstack lateral cisterna invents a route the text does not describe—export occurs specifically at the trans face (line 5771), not from intermediate cisternae. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the cis/trans polarity rule to a tracked-protein scenario and infer which face serves entry versus exit rather than recalling a definition in isolation.$ex19$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc19$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc19$, 'biology', 2, 100
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a19A$Swaps cis and trans, treating the trans face as the ER-receiving side$a19A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a19B$Correct entry face but wrongly makes the same cis face also serve as the exit, collapsing the stack's polarity$a19B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a19D$Reverses entry direction and invents a mid-stack lateral budding site instead of trans-face export$a19D$ FROM q;

-- Q11 [Golgi Apparatus · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g20)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Golgi Apparatus',
    $qt20$A fibroblast carries a loss-of-function mutation in GlcNAc-phosphotransferase, the Golgi enzyme that initiates the mannose-6-phosphate tag on newly synthesized acid hydrolases. Assuming the cell's pH gradients and bulk secretory flow are otherwise normal, where do these acid hydrolases most likely end up?$qt20$,
    $op20$[{"label":"A","text":"Retained in the rough endoplasmic reticulum lumen, because without the tag they cannot exit toward the Golgi cis face"},{"label":"B","text":"Delivered to lysosomes but catalytically inactive, because the missing tag is required to acidify the lysosomal lumen"},{"label":"C","text":"Routed into mitochondria, because untagged hydrolytic enzymes default to the organelle bearing its own import machinery"},{"label":"D","text":"Secreted from the cell at the plasma membrane, because untagged hydrolases default into the trans-Golgi secretory pathway"}]$op20$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex20$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because the mannose-6-phosphate tag is the address label that diverts acid hydrolases out of the default trans-Golgi secretory stream and into lysosome-bound vesicles, so without it the enzymes follow the bulk secretory route to the plasma membrane and exit the cell. In the Golgi, proteins are sorted by "adding short sugar molecule chains" and then "tag[ged] with phosphate groups or other small molecules in order to travel to their proper destinations," and "the modified and tagged proteins are packaged into secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi's trans face," where some "deposit their contents into other cell parts" while "other secretory vesicles fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell" (lines 5766-5773). The phosphate tag is precisely what selects the lysosomal branch over the secretory branch; remove the tagging step and the lysosomal hydrolases are read as default secretory cargo and exported, never reaching the lysosome (lines 5815-5822). (Choice A) Incorrect: this confuses the Golgi sorting tag with the ER-exit step. The textbook places sugar/phosphate tagging in the Golgi, and proteins "not destined to stay in the RER ... reach their destinations via transport vesicles that bud from the RER's membrane" regardless of the later mannose-6-phosphate label, so ER exit is unaffected by this Golgi enzyme. (Choice B) Incorrect: this is a partial-truth pH trap that swaps the tag's actual job (sorting/addressing) for lumen acidification; the stem also stipulates pH gradients are normal, and the tag's role per the Golgi passage is to direct cargo to "their proper destinations," not to lower lysosomal pH. (Choice C) Incorrect: mitochondrial membranes are explicitly excluded from the endomembrane system (line 5653), and mitochondrial protein import uses its own targeting sequences, not a Golgi default, so untagged secretory-pathway hydrolases cannot be routed there. (Choice D) Correct: with the phosphate address removed, the hydrolases are handled as ordinary secretory cargo and follow the trans-Golgi vesicles that "fuse with the plasma membrane and release their contents outside the cell." This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the loss of a specific sorting tag to a predicted change in destination using the Golgi's tag-then-sort pathway.$ex20$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc20$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc20$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'process_step_confusion', $a20A$Misplaces the failed step at ER exit rather than Golgi sorting$a20A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'partial_truth', $a20B$pH/acidification trap that reassigns the tag's role from addressing to lumen acidification$a20B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a20C$Untagged enzyme defaults to the self-importing organelle (mitochondria)$a20C$ FROM q;

-- Q12 [Lysosomes · medium · Skill 2 · ans A] (g4)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Lysosomes',
    $qt4$A drug blocks the proton pump in the membrane of a macrophage's lysosomes, so the lysosomal lumen equilibrates to the same near-neutral pH as the surrounding cytoplasm. Which outcome most directly follows for material the cell has engulfed?$qt4$,
    $op4$[{"label":"A","text":"Engulfed macromolecules accumulate undigested because the acid hydrolases lose catalytic activity at the now near-neutral pH."},{"label":"B","text":"Engulfed macromolecules are degraded faster because the higher pH relieves acid inhibition of the hydrolases."},{"label":"C","text":"Engulfed macromolecules accumulate because the acid hydrolases are permanently denatured and destroyed by the pH change."},{"label":"D","text":"Engulfed macromolecules are repackaged into vesicles and exported because peroxisomal oxidation replaces lysosomal digestion."}]$op4$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex4$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because lysosomal hydrolases are only active at the low pH the proton pump maintains, so neutralizing the lumen leaves them inactive and substrate undigested. MAIN REASONING: OpenStax states that the enzymes within lysosomes break down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids, and worn-out organelles, and that these enzymes "are active at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm's. Therefore, the pH within lysosomes is more acidic than the cytoplasm's pH" (lines 5549-5556). The acidic lumen is generated by an active proton pump; if that pump is blocked, the lumen drifts toward the cytoplasm's near-neutral pH \((\sim 7)\), which is outside the operating range of the acid hydrolases. The text emphasizes that "Many reactions that take place in the cytoplasm could not occur at a low pH," underscoring that these enzymes are pH-tuned to their compartment, so removing the acidity shuts down digestion and engulfed substrate piles up rather than being broken down. (Choice A) is correct: the loss of digestion follows directly from inactivation of pH-dependent hydrolases, not from any change to the enzymes' structure; raising the pump back would restore activity, which is the signature of reversible loss of activity rather than destruction. (Choice B) reverses the relationship — the hydrolases require the low pH, so a higher pH suppresses rather than enhances their activity; the claim that acidity "inhibits" them inverts the actual pH optimum described at lines 5555-5556. (Choice C) is a partial truth taken too far: digestion does halt, but the enzymes are inactivated by being outside their pH optimum, not permanently denatured and destroyed; the textbook attributes lysosomal function to a low-pH requirement (lines 5555-5556), not to irreversible destruction of the proteins. (Choice D) imports the wrong organelle: peroxisomes carry out oxidation of fatty acids and amino acids and detoxify \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) (lines 5508-5511), and they do not substitute for lysosomal hydrolytic digestion of engulfed macromolecules, so no peroxisome-driven rescue or export occurs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must combine the proton pump's role in acidifying the lumen with the hydrolases' pH dependence to predict the functional consequence of the inhibitor.$ex4$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc4$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc4$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a4B$Treats higher pH as relieving inhibition rather than removing the required acidic optimum$a4B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a4C$Confuses loss of activity outside pH optimum with permanent denaturation/destruction of the enzyme$a4C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a4D$Substitutes peroxisomal oxidation for lysosomal hydrolytic digestion$a4D$ FROM q;

-- Q13 [Lysosomes · easy · Skill 1 · ans C] (g21)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Lysosomes',
    $qt21$A neuron accumulates a damaged, nonfunctional mitochondrion and must break it down so its building-block molecules can be reused. The organelle that most directly carries out this digestion and recycling is the:$qt21$,
    $op21$[{"label":"A","text":"peroxisome, which uses oxidative enzymes to break down fatty acids and detoxify \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2\\)"},{"label":"B","text":"mitochondrion, which uses its inner-membrane cristae to oxidize substrates during cellular respiration"},{"label":"C","text":"lysosome, which uses acid hydrolases to digest macromolecules and worn-out organelles"},{"label":"D","text":"rough endoplasmic reticulum, which uses membrane-bound ribosomes to synthesize and process proteins"}]$op21$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex21$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the lysosome is the animal cell's digestive organelle, using hydrolytic enzymes active at low pH to break down macromolecules and degrade worn-out organelles for recycling. The textbook describes lysosomes as the cell's \"garbage disposal,\" stating that \"Enzymes within the lysosomes aid in breaking down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids, and even worn-out organelles\" and that these enzymes are \"active at a much lower pH than the cytoplasm's\" (lines 5549–5555). The chapter summary reinforces that \"Lysosomes digest macromolecules, recycle worn-out organelles, and destroy pathogens\" (lines 6324–6326), which is exactly the autophagic recycling of the damaged mitochondrion described in the stem. (Choice A) The peroxisome is incorrect; the textbook states peroxisomes \"carry out oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids and amino acids\" and detoxify poisons, safely breaking \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) into oxygen and water (lines 5508–5511) — they perform oxidative metabolism, not bulk hydrolytic digestion of whole organelles. (Choice B) The mitochondrion is incorrect; its cristae and matrix function in cellular respiration to produce ATP (lines 5485–5488), so it is the organelle being recycled here, not the one doing the recycling. (Choice C) is correct; the lysosome's acid hydrolases digest macromolecules and worn-out organelles, matching the autophagic breakdown in the stem (lines 5549–5550, 6324–6326). (Choice D) The rough endoplasmic reticulum is incorrect; its membrane-bound ribosomes synthesize and process proteins for the endomembrane system, not degrade organelles — it builds molecules rather than breaking them down. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the lysosome is the membrane-bound organelle whose acid hydrolases digest macromolecules and recycle worn-out organelles via autophagy.$ex21$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc21$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc21$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a21A$Confusing the peroxisome's oxidative breakdown of fatty acids and H2O2 detoxification with the lysosome's hydrolytic digestion$a21A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'reversed_relationship', $a21B$Naming the very organelle being recycled (mitochondrion) as the recycler$a21B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'adjacent_fact', $a21D$Confusing the rough ER's protein-synthesis role with the lysosome's protein-degradation role$a21D$ FROM q;

-- Q14 [Lysosomes · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g22)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Lysosomes',
    $qt22$An infant inherits a defect that eliminates a single lysosomal acid hydrolase whose only substrate is the membrane lipid GM2 ganglioside; all other lysosomal hydrolases remain fully functional. Neurons, which turn over large amounts of this lipid, progressively deteriorate. Which outcome most directly explains the neuronal dysfunction?$qt22$,
    $op22$[{"label":"A","text":"GM2 ganglioside cannot be oxidized in peroxisomes, so its breakdown products accumulate as toxic \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2\\) in the cytoplasm."},{"label":"B","text":"Undigested GM2 ganglioside accumulates inside engorged lysosomes because no other hydrolase can cleave it, disrupting neuronal function."},{"label":"C","text":"All lysosomal digestion halts because the missing hydrolase is required to activate the remaining acid hydrolases, leaving every substrate undigested."},{"label":"D","text":"GM2 ganglioside leaks into the cytoplasm, where its breakdown proceeds normally but raises cytoplasmic pH above that of the lysosome."}]$op22$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex22$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the loss of one acid hydrolase blocks digestion of only that enzyme's specific substrate, so GM2 ganglioside builds up undigested and swells the lysosomes that should have cleared it. Lysosomes are the cell's "garbage disposal," and the enzymes within them break down proteins, polysaccharides, lipids, nucleic acids, and worn-out organelles (lines 5548-5550). Each acid hydrolase is substrate-specific, so eliminating the one enzyme that cleaves GM2 ganglioside leaves that single lipid undigested while the cell's other hydrolases keep clearing their own targets. In a neuron, which turns over large amounts of this membrane lipid, the undigested substrate accumulates inside the lysosomes, engorging them and impairing the cell. The defect is therefore a targeted failure to digest one substrate, not a global shutdown of lysosomal digestion. (Choice A) This commits an organelle mix-up: peroxisomes, not lysosomes, carry out oxidation reactions that break down fatty acids and produce \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) (lines 5508-5511), and the scenario specifies a lysosomal hydrolase, so peroxisomal oxidation and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) are irrelevant to GM2 clearance. (Choice B) Correct: because lysosomal hydrolases are substrate-specific, loss of the single GM2-cleaving enzyme leaves that lipid undigested while the other hydrolases continue functioning, so the lipid accumulates inside swollen lysosomes and disrupts the neuron. (Choice C) This overgeneralizes a single-enzyme deficiency into a total digestive arrest; the stem states the remaining acid hydrolases are fully functional, and the text describes lysosomal enzymes breaking down many distinct macromolecule classes (lines 5549-5550), with no enzyme serving to "activate" the others. (Choice D) This reverses the pH relationship and misplaces digestion: the text states lysosomal enzymes are active at a much lower pH and that the lysosomal interior is more acidic than the cytoplasm (lines 5554-5556), so these acid hydrolases would not function at cytoplasmic pH, and digestion does not proceed normally there. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a single-enzyme deficiency to the substrate-specific accumulation it causes and reject the overgeneralized claim that all digestion stops.$ex22$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc22$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc22$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a22A$Attributes the digestion failure to the wrong organelle (peroxisomal fatty-acid oxidation and H2O2) instead of the lysosome named in the stem.$a22A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a22C$Overgeneralizes a single missing hydrolase into a complete shutdown of all lysosomal digestion via an invented activation requirement.$a22C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'reversed_relationship', $a22D$Reverses the lysosome-vs-cytoplasm pH relationship and wrongly claims acid hydrolase digestion proceeds normally at cytoplasmic pH.$a22D$ FROM q;

-- Q15 [Mitochondria · medium · Skill 2 · ans A] (g2)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Mitochondria',
    $qt2$A small-molecule drug binds an inner-mitochondrial-membrane shaping protein in cultured muscle cells, causing the cristae to flatten so that the inner membrane becomes smooth. Assuming the outer membrane, matrix volume, and mitochondrial DNA are unaffected, which outcome most directly follows from this change?$qt2$,
    $op2$[{"label":"A","text":"ATP output declines because the reduced inner-membrane surface area provides less room for the electron transport chain."},{"label":"B","text":"Citric-acid-cycle flux declines because the matrix loses the surface that anchors its dehydrogenase enzymes."},{"label":"C","text":"Metabolite import declines because the smoothed outer membrane has fewer porins for small molecules to cross."},{"label":"D","text":"Mitochondrial replication declines because flattened cristae can no longer hold the mitochondrial DNA."}]$op2$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex2$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because cristae are folds that increase the inner-membrane surface area, and that membrane is where ATP synthesis occurs, so flattening the cristae shrinks the working surface and lowers ATP output. The textbook states that mitochondria are "double membrane organelles" whose "inner layer has folds called cristae," that these folds "increase its surface area," and that "ATP synthesis takes place on the inner membrane" (lines 5485-5495). The electron transport chain and ATP synthase are embedded in this inner membrane, so the amount of inner-membrane surface area sets a ceiling on how much of that machinery a mitochondrion can hold; smoothing the cristae directly reduces that area and therefore the capacity to generate ATP via the ETC. Because the prompt holds the outer membrane, matrix volume, and \(\text{mtDNA}\) constant, the most direct consequence is isolated to the inner-membrane respiratory machinery. (Choice A) Correct: the cristae exist specifically to "increase its surface area" for ATP synthesis "on the inner membrane," so reducing the folds removes inner-membrane real estate for the electron transport chain and \(\text{ATP}\) synthase, lowering ATP output even though every other component is intact. (Choice B) The citric acid cycle runs in the soluble mitochondrial matrix, which the textbook describes as a separate compartment ("the area surrounded by the folds the mitochondrial matrix"); its enzymes are dissolved in the matrix rather than anchored to the cristae, and the stem holds matrix volume constant, so cycle flux is not the direct casualty of flattening the folds. (Choice C) Porins reside in the outer membrane, but the stem explicitly states the outer membrane is unaffected and the drug acts on an inner-membrane shaping protein, so import capacity is unchanged; this choice swaps the affected membrane for the wrong one. (Choice D) Although mitochondria do "have their own ribosomes and DNA," the \(\text{mtDNA}\) sits in the matrix and the stem stipulates it is unaffected; cristae do not tether or replicate the genome, so linking fold loss to replication confuses an unrelated mitochondrial feature with the membrane's surface-area role. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must connect the structural role of cristae (inner-membrane surface area) to the functional location of the electron transport chain and predict the most direct downstream effect.$ex2$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc2$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc2$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a2B$Attributes the loss to citric-acid-cycle (matrix) enzymes, a real respiration step located in a different compartment than the cristae.$a2B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a2C$Moves the effect to the outer membrane and porins, an actual structure but the wrong membrane given the drug's inner-membrane target.$a2C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a2D$Links cristae to mtDNA replication, falsely treating the folds as the genome's anchor when mtDNA resides in the matrix.$a2D$ FROM q;

-- Q16 [Mitochondria · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g12)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Mitochondria',
    $qt12$A researcher tags an enzyme of the citric acid cycle in a respiring liver cell and tracks where it carries out its reaction. The reaction this enzyme catalyzes is most directly localized to which mitochondrial compartment?$qt12$,
    $op12$[{"label":"A","text":"The inner mitochondrial membrane, where the cristae folds reside"},{"label":"B","text":"The mitochondrial matrix, the space enclosed by the inner membrane"},{"label":"C","text":"The intermembrane space, between the outer and inner membranes"},{"label":"D","text":"The outer mitochondrial membrane, which bounds the organelle"}]$op12$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex12$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because the citric acid cycle, including its enzymes, takes place in the mitochondrial matrix, the space enclosed by the inner membrane. The mitochondrion is a double-membrane organelle whose inner membrane is folded into cristae; the space enclosed by that inner membrane is the matrix, and the space between the two membranes is the intermembrane space (lines 5485-5495). The textbook states that "the citric acid cycle takes place in the matrix of mitochondria" and that "almost all of the enzymes of the citric acid cycle are soluble" within that compartment (lines 9389-9390). This is functionally separate from the inner membrane, where ATP synthesis and the proton gradient of the electron transport chain operate (line 5495). (Choice B) Correct: the soluble citric-acid-cycle enzymes reside in and act within the matrix, the compartment the inner membrane encloses; the lone membrane-bound exception, succinate dehydrogenase, does not change the cycle's matrix localization. (Choice A) Incorrect (adjacent_fact): the inner membrane and its cristae host the electron transport chain and ATP synthase that build and use the proton gradient, not the soluble cycle reactions, so this confuses the membrane-bound oxidative phosphorylation machinery with the matrix-localized cycle. (Choice C) Incorrect (misconception): the intermembrane space is the reservoir into which protons \(\left(\text{H}^+\right)\) are pumped to form the gradient, not a catalytic compartment for the cycle's soluble enzymes. (Choice D) Incorrect (misconception): the outer membrane merely bounds the organelle and has no role in housing citric-acid-cycle reactions, which require the enclosed matrix interior. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the internal organization of the mitochondrion and which sub-process of aerobic respiration each compartment carries out.$ex12$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc12$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc12$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a12A$Places the cycle on the inner membrane/cristae, conflating the ETC and ATP synthase with the soluble matrix enzymes$a12A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'misconception', $a12C$Places the cycle in the intermembrane space, confusing the proton-gradient reservoir with the catalytic compartment$a12C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a12D$Places the cycle on the outer boundary membrane, which has no respiratory enzymatic role$a12D$ FROM q;

-- Q17 [Mitochondria · easy · Skill 1 · ans C] (g13)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Mitochondria',
    $qt13$A researcher loads a cultured cell with a fluorescent dye that, by design, accumulates only in the innermost mitochondrial compartment — the space fully enclosed by the inner membrane, where the citric acid cycle enzymes are dissolved. After imaging, the dye marks one specific compartment. Which compartment is labeled?$qt13$,
    $op13$[{"label":"A","text":"The intermembrane space, the region lying between the outer and inner membranes"},{"label":"B","text":"The cytosol, the gel-like region surrounding the entire mitochondrion"},{"label":"C","text":"The matrix, the space enclosed by the inner membrane"},{"label":"D","text":"The lumen inside the cristae folds, a compartment separate from the central interior"}]$op13$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex13$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the matrix is, by definition, the space enclosed by the inner membrane, and it is where the citric-acid-cycle enzymes are located. The text states that the inner layer of the double membrane has folds called cristae and identifies "the space inside the inner membrane the mitochondrial matrix," distinguishing it from "the space between the two membranes," which is the intermembrane space. Because the dye is described as accumulating in the innermost compartment fully enclosed by the inner membrane — the home of the citric acid cycle enzymes — it must be marking the matrix. (Choice A) The intermembrane space is the compartment "between the two membranes," lying outside the inner membrane rather than enclosed by it, so it is not the innermost compartment. (Choice B) The cytosol is the cell's "gel-like" material surrounding organelles, located outside the mitochondrion entirely between the plasma membrane and nuclear envelope, not inside the inner membrane. (Choice C) Correct: the matrix is explicitly defined as "the space inside the inner membrane," and the matrix is "rich with the enzymes necessary for aerobic respiration," matching the dye's target. (Choice D) The cristae are folds of the inner membrane that increase its surface area; the text does not describe their interior as a separate enclosed lumen distinct from the matrix, so treating it as its own compartment is incorrect. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how the mitochondrion's membranes partition its interior into named compartments.$ex13$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc13$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc13$, 'biology', 1, 45
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a13A$Names a real, adjacent mitochondrial compartment (intermembrane space) that is between the membranes rather than enclosed by the inner membrane$a13A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'scale_unit_error', $a13B$Confuses a compartment outside the organelle (the surrounding cytosol) with an internal mitochondrial compartment$a13B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a13D$Treats the cristae folds as a separate enclosed lumen distinct from the matrix rather than infoldings of the inner membrane$a13D$ FROM q;

-- Q18 [Mitochondria · medium · Skill 2 · ans D] (g14)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Mitochondria',
    $qt14$In a family, an affected mother transmits a particular trait to every one of her sons and daughters, but an affected father transmits it to none of his children. Which inheritance pattern most directly explains this transmission?$qt14$,
    $op14$[{"label":"A","text":"Autosomal dominant inheritance of a gene on a nuclear chromosome"},{"label":"B","text":"X-linked dominant inheritance of a gene on the nuclear X chromosome"},{"label":"C","text":"Autosomal recessive inheritance of a gene on a nuclear chromosome"},{"label":"D","text":"Maternal (cytoplasmic) inheritance of a gene on mitochondrial DNA"}]$op14$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex14$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because mitochondria carry their own DNA and are delivered to the zygote almost entirely through the egg cytoplasm, so only mothers transmit a mitochondrially encoded trait, and they transmit it to all offspring regardless of sex. The textbook states that mitochondria "have their own ribosomes and DNA" (line 5485), which is genetic material physically separate from the nuclear DNA the nucleus "houses" (line 5398). Because the sperm contributes essentially no cytoplasm to the zygote, the offspring's mitochondria (and the alleles they carry) come from the egg; an affected mother therefore passes the trait to every child, while an affected father passes it to none. This maternal, cytoplasmic transmission is non-Mendelian precisely because the responsible gene lies outside the nuclear chromosomes that segregate during meiosis. (Choice A) Autosomal dominant inheritance involves a nuclear chromosome gene transmitted equally through eggs and sperm, so an affected father would pass it to about half his children—contradicting the observation that he transmits to none. (Choice B) X-linked dominant inheritance is also nuclear and would let an affected father transmit the trait to all of his daughters (who receive his X), which is not seen here. (Choice C) Autosomal recessive inheritance is nuclear and would not produce transmission exclusively through the mother; both parents contribute the gene, and the recessive phenotype typically skips generations rather than passing from one affected parent to every child. (Choice D) Maternal cytoplasmic inheritance correctly accounts for transmission only from the mother to all offspring, because the relevant gene resides on mitochondrial DNA delivered via the egg cytoplasm; the nuance is that the pattern is sex-independent in offspring but strictly sex-dependent in the transmitting parent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the fact that mitochondria carry their own DNA inherited through the egg cytoplasm to predict a non-Mendelian pedigree pattern.$ex14$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc14$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc14$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a14A$Treats an extranuclear cytoplasmic pattern as ordinary nuclear autosomal dominant inheritance, ignoring that an affected father would then transmit to ~half his children$a14A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a14B$Confuses maternal mitochondrial transmission with X-linked dominant inheritance, which is also nuclear and would let an affected father transmit to all daughters$a14B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a14C$Invokes autosomal recessive nuclear inheritance, conflating 'comes from the mother' with a recessive pattern that requires contributions from both parents and skips generations$a14C$ FROM q;

-- Q19 [Mitochondria · hard · Skill 2 · ans B] (g15)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Mitochondria',
    $qt15$A chloramphenicol-class antibiotic that selectively binds bacterial-type ribosomes is added to cultured human cells. Given that mitochondria possess their own ribosomes and DNA, which translation pool is most directly impaired, and why?$qt15$,
    $op15$[{"label":"A","text":"Cytoplasmic translation, because the free and ER-bound ribosomes that build most cellular proteins are bacterial in type"},{"label":"B","text":"Mitochondrial translation, because the organelle's own ribosomes resemble those of bacteria and are the antibiotic's target"},{"label":"C","text":"Both pools equally, because the cytoplasmic ribosomes that supply mitochondrial proteins are identical to the mitochondrial ribosomes"},{"label":"D","text":"Mitochondrial translation, because the antibiotic enters the matrix and degrades the circular mitochondrial DNA template directly"}]$op15$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex15$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because mitochondria possess their own ribosomes that are bacteria-like, so an antibiotic specific for bacterial-type ribosomes blocks translation inside the mitochondrion while leaving cytoplasmic ribosomes untouched. OpenStax notes that "mitochondria are oval-shaped, double membrane organelles that have their own ribosomes and DNA" (line 5485), a set of features shared with prokaryotes, which "have chromosomal DNA localized in a nucleoid, ribosomes" (line 5223-5224). The cell's main protein-synthesis machinery is the cytoplasmic ribosome population that "float freely in the cytoplasm" or attach to the ER, receiving "orders... from the nucleus" (lines 5450-5454); these eukaryotic ribosomes differ from the bacterial-type ribosomes inside mitochondria, so a drug specific for bacterial ribosomes selectively impairs the mitochondrial pool. Because the antibiotic acts on the ribosome (translation), not on the DNA template, the circular mitochondrial genome itself is not destroyed. (Choice A) This reverses the relationship: the free and ER-bound cytoplasmic ribosomes are the eukaryotic type that synthesize most cellular proteins (lines 5449-5454), not bacterial-type, so they are not the antibiotic's target. (Choice B) Correct; the mitochondrion's own ribosomes (line 5485) are bacteria-like, consistent with the organelle's prokaryotic ancestry, making the mitochondrial translation pool the selective target while cytoplasmic ribosomes continue working. (Choice C) This is a partial truth: it is true that nucleus-encoded mitochondrial proteins are translated on cytoplasmic ribosomes, but those cytoplasmic ribosomes are eukaryotic, not "identical to" the bacterial-type mitochondrial ribosomes, so the two pools are not blocked equally. (Choice D) This is a process-step confusion: the antibiotic class targets the ribosome to halt translation, not the DNA, so it does not degrade the circular mitochondrial genome (line 5485 establishes the DNA as a separate component from the ribosomes). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the shared bacteria-like features of mitochondria to predict which of two translation systems a bacterial-ribosome-specific drug will disrupt.$ex15$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc15$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc15$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a15A$Assigning the bacterial-type ribosome identity to the cytoplasmic pool instead of the mitochondrial pool$a15A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a15C$Correctly recalling that cytoplasmic ribosomes make nucleus-encoded mito proteins but wrongly equating them with mitochondrial ribosomes, predicting equal blockade$a15C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a15D$Confusing a translation-targeting antibiotic with a DNA-degrading mechanism on the circular mitochondrial genome$a15D$ FROM q;

-- Q20 [Nucleus · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g1)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Nucleus',
    $qt1$A cell synthesizes a large transcription factor protein on free ribosomes in the cytoplasm. For this protein to regulate gene expression, it must reach the chromatin inside the nucleus. Which structure most directly mediates the protein's entry into the nucleus?$qt1$,
    $op1$[{"label":"A","text":"The nucleolus within the nucleoplasm"},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear pores spanning the nuclear envelope"},{"label":"C","text":"The double phospholipid bilayer of the nuclear envelope"},{"label":"D","text":"Free diffusion across the inner nuclear membrane"}]$op1$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex1$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because nuclear pores are the gated channels that control the passage of large molecules such as proteins and RNA between the nucleoplasm and the cytoplasm. MAIN REASONING: The nuclear envelope is a double-membrane barrier, and its two phospholipid bilayers are impermeable to large macromolecules; the textbook states that \"the nuclear envelope is punctuated with pores that control the passage of ions, molecules, and RNA between the nucleoplasm and cytoplasm\" (lines 5413-5414). A cytoplasmically synthesized transcription factor is far too large and polar to cross the lipid bilayer, so it must travel through these pores, which provide regulated two-way macromolecular exchange (line 5407: \"Nuclear pores allow substances to enter and exit the nucleus\"). (Choice A) The nucleolus is a darkly staining region inside the nucleus that aggregates ribosomal RNA with proteins to assemble ribosomal subunits (lines 5442-5446); it is a site of rRNA processing and ribosome assembly, not a transport gateway across the nuclear boundary, so it does not mediate the protein's entry. (Choice B) Correct. Nuclear pores are the selective gatekeepers of the nuclear envelope, mediating regulated bidirectional transport of large molecules; the same pores that import this protein also export assembled ribosomal subunits and RNA outward (lines 5413-5414, 5446). (Choice C) The double phospholipid bilayer of the nuclear envelope is precisely the barrier that the protein cannot cross; both its inner and outer membranes are phospholipid bilayers (lines 5410-5411), and the hydrophobic bilayer interior blocks large polar macromolecules rather than admitting them. (Choice D) Free diffusion across the bilayer is impossible for a large protein because the membrane is selectively permeable and its interior is hydrophobic (lines 6521, 6602-6607); only small, nonpolar molecules cross freely, so an unaided diffusion mechanism cannot deliver the transcription factor. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the gatekeeping function of nuclear pores and the impermeability of the nuclear envelope's bilayers to large macromolecules.$ex1$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc1$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc1$, 'biology', 1, 75
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'adjacent_fact', $a1A$naming a different nuclear sub-structure (nucleolus / rRNA synthesis) that performs an unrelated function$a1A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'partial_truth', $a1C$identifying the correct organelle boundary but the component that blocks rather than admits the protein$a1C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'misconception', $a1D$assuming large macromolecules can freely diffuse across a selectively permeable bilayer$a1D$ FROM q;

-- Q21 [Nucleus · easy · Skill 1 · ans B] (g9)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Nucleus',
    $qt9$A researcher isolates the genetic material from inside the nucleus of a human liver cell and finds that the DNA is not free-floating but is tightly associated with a set of small basic proteins. Given this finding, how is the DNA organized within the nucleus?$qt9$,
    $op9$[{"label":"A","text":"It exists as a single naked circular molecule floating freely in the nucleoplasm."},{"label":"B","text":"It is wound around histone proteins to form chromatin, which makes up the chromosomes."},{"label":"C","text":"It is stored as a double-stranded RNA molecule rather than as DNA."},{"label":"D","text":"It is associated with these proteins but remains permanently uncondensed, never forming chromosomes."}]$op9$::jsonb,
    'B',
    $ex9$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is B because in eukaryotic cells the DNA complexes with histone proteins to form chromatin, the material that makes up the chromosomes. The textbook states that "In eukaryotic cells but not in prokaryotes, DNA forms a complex with histone proteins to form chromatin, the substance of eukaryotic chromosomes," and that the nucleus "stores chromatin (DNA plus proteins) in a gel-like substance called the nucleoplasm." The small basic proteins the researcher finds bound to the DNA are these histones, and the protein-DNA complex is chromatin, which can condense into visible chromosomes when the cell prepares to divide. (Choice A) A single naked circular molecule describes the prokaryotic arrangement; the text notes that "in prokaryotes, DNA is organized into a single circular chromosome. In eukaryotes, chromosomes are linear structures," and human nuclear DNA is bound to proteins, not naked. (Choice B) Correct: linear DNA wraps around histones to form chromatin/chromosomes. (Choice C) DNA, not RNA, is the genetic material stored in the nucleus; the text states "DNA is the genetic material in all living organisms" and that the nucleus "houses the cell's DNA." (Choice D) The protein-DNA complex (chromatin) is precisely the material of the chromosomes; the text says chromatin "describes the material that makes up the chromosomes both when condensed and decondensed," so it does form chromosomes rather than remaining permanently uncondensed. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how eukaryotic nuclear DNA is packaged with histone proteins into chromatin and chromosomes.$ex9$,
    'easy', '2A', $cc9$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc9$, 'biology', 1, 60
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a9A$Applying the prokaryotic naked-circular-chromosome arrangement to a eukaryotic nucleus$a9A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'adjacent_fact', $a9C$Confusing the nucleic acid type, treating stored genetic material as RNA rather than DNA$a9C$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'partial_truth', $a9D$Accepting the DNA-protein association but denying that chromatin is the material of chromosomes$a9D$ FROM q;

-- Q22 [Nucleus · medium · Skill 2 · ans C] (g10)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Nucleus',
    $qt10$A ribosome studding the outer membrane of the nuclear envelope co-translationally threads a newly made membrane protein into the lumen of the perinuclear space. The route by which this protein's lumenal domain reaches the lumen of the rough endoplasmic reticulum is BEST explained by which feature?$qt10$,
    $op10$[{"label":"A","text":"Transport vesicles bud from the outer nuclear membrane and fuse with the rough ER, delivering the protein to the ER lumen"},{"label":"B","text":"Nuclear pores conduct the protein from the perinuclear space into the cytoplasm and then into the rough ER lumen"},{"label":"C","text":"The outer nuclear membrane is physically continuous with the rough ER membrane, so the perinuclear space is contiguous with the ER lumen"},{"label":"D","text":"The nucleolus assembles the protein into a ribosomal subunit that is exported through the nuclear envelope to the rough ER"}]$op10$::jsonb,
    'C',
    $ex10$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is C because the outer nuclear membrane and the rough ER membrane form one continuous bilayer, making the perinuclear space and the ER lumen a single connected compartment that the protein's lumenal domain enters directly. The nuclear envelope's outer membrane is studded with ribosomes and is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum (the textbook states "The nuclear membrane is continuous with the endoplasmic reticulum" and "The ER's membrane... is continuous with the nuclear envelope"). When a ribosome on this outer membrane co-translationally inserts a protein, the protein's lumenal portion enters the perinuclear space, which is the same compartment as the ER lumen because the two membranes are physically joined; no membrane fusion or vesicle is needed to cross between them. This is the same lumen into which RER ribosomes transfer their newly synthesized proteins ("Ribosomes transfer their newly synthesized proteins into the RER's lumen"). (Choice A) Vesicle budding and fusion is the mechanism for moving cargo between separate, discontinuous compartments (for example, from the RER to the Golgi); it is unnecessary and incorrect here because the outer nuclear membrane and ER membrane are already one continuous structure, not two compartments requiring a vesicle bridge. (Choice B) Nuclear pores regulate passage of ions, molecules, and RNA between the nucleoplasm and the cytoplasm across the nuclear envelope; they do not connect the perinuclear/ER lumen to the cytoplasm, and a co-translationally inserted lumenal protein never travels through a pore. (Choice C) Correct: continuity of the outer nuclear membrane with the rough ER means the perinuclear space and ER lumen are one contiguous lumen, so the lumenal domain arrives without any transport step; this exploits the structural relationship rather than an active transport pathway. (Choice D) The nucleolus aggregates ribosomal RNA with proteins to assemble ribosomal subunits that exit through nuclear pores; it does not package secretory or membrane proteins for delivery to the ER, so this confuses ribosome biogenesis with the routing of a co-translationally inserted protein. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structural fact of nuclear-envelope/ER membrane continuity to a specific protein-routing situation and infer that no vesicular transport step is required.$ex10$,
    'medium', '2A', $cc10$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc10$, 'biology', 2, 105
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'misconception', $a10A$Assumes all inter-organelle movement requires vesicle budding/fusion, overlooking direct membrane continuity$a10A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a10B$Invokes nuclear pores (a real envelope feature) for a lumenal protein that pores never handle$a10B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'D', 'process_step_confusion', $a10D$Conflates nucleolar ribosome-subunit assembly/export with secretory protein routing into the ER$a10D$ FROM q;

-- Q23 [Nucleus · hard · Skill 2 · ans D] (g11)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Nucleus',
    $qt11$A toxin selectively binds proteins in the nucleolus and halts the aggregation of ribosomal RNA with associated proteins into ribosomal subunits, while leaving transcription of messenger RNA, the nuclear pores, and the cytoplasm's existing ribosomes structurally intact. As the cell's pool of mature cytoplasmic ribosomes is gradually depleted over time, which outcome most directly explains the resulting decline in the cell's protein-synthesis capacity?$qt11$,
    $op11$[{"label":"A","text":"Messenger RNA can no longer be transcribed from DNA, so no translatable code reaches the cytoplasm."},{"label":"B","text":"The nuclear pores close, so messenger RNA is trapped in the nucleoplasm and cannot reach the cytoplasm."},{"label":"C","text":"Existing cytoplasmic ribosomes are chemically inactivated, so they can no longer translate messenger RNA into protein."},{"label":"D","text":"Fewer ribosomal subunits are assembled and exported through the nuclear pores, so the cytoplasm's supply of functional ribosomes available for translation falls."}]$op11$::jsonb,
    'D',
    $ex11$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is D because the nucleolus assembles ribosomal subunits that are exported through the nuclear pores to the cytoplasm, so blocking that assembly starves the cytoplasm of the ribosomes that translation requires. The textbook states that the nucleolus \"aggregates the ribosomal RNA with associated proteins to assemble the ribosomal subunits that are then transported out through the pores in the nuclear envelope to the cytoplasm\" (lines 5444-5446), and that \"Ribosomes are the cellular structures responsible for protein synthesis\" which \"translate the code provided by the sequence of the nitrogenous bases in the mRNA into a specific order of amino acids in a protein\" (lines 5449, 5455-5456). Halting nucleolar subunit assembly does not stop transcription or pore transport directly; instead it cuts off the resupply of new subunits, so as existing cytoplasmic ribosomes are depleted, the number of ribosomes available to translate \(\text{mRNA}\) falls and total translational capacity declines. (Choice A) This reverses the affected step: the stem specifies that \(\text{mRNA}\) transcription remains intact, and transcription is directed by chromosomal DNA in the nucleus, not by the nucleolus, which assembles ribosomal subunits (lines 5444-5446, 5454-5455). (Choice B) The nuclear pores are explicitly stated to remain intact and they \"control the passage of ions, molecules, and RNA between the nucleoplasm and cytoplasm\" (lines 5413-5414); the toxin acts on nucleolar assembly, not on pore patency, so \(\text{mRNA}\) export is not blocked. (Choice C) This misplaces the lesion: the toxin inactivates nucleolar assembly proteins, not the mature cytoplasmic ribosomes, which the stem says are left structurally intact and which remain capable of translation until they are gradually depleted (lines 5449-5456). (Choice D) This is correct because nucleolar subunit assembly feeds the export of new subunits through the pores, and once that pipeline is blocked the finite cytoplasmic ribosome pool runs down, directly lowering the cell's translational capacity (lines 5444-5446). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain a block at one upstream step (nucleolar subunit assembly) through subunit export to its delayed downstream effect on cytoplasmic ribosome supply and translation, rather than recall an isolated fact.$ex11$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc11$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc11$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
INSERT INTO question_distractor_metadata (question_id, choice_label, distractor_category, archetype)
SELECT id, 'A', 'reversed_relationship', $a11A$Attributes the lesion to mRNA transcription (a DNA/chromosomal step) rather than nucleolar subunit assembly, confusing which nuclear process the toxin actually halts$a11A$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'B', 'adjacent_fact', $a11B$Invokes a real nuclear-pore function (RNA export) that is explicitly preserved in the stem, mislocating the block to pore transport$a11B$ FROM q
UNION ALL SELECT id, 'C', 'process_step_confusion', $a11C$Places the lesion on mature cytoplasmic ribosomes (the translation machinery) instead of on the upstream assembly of new subunits in the nucleolus$a11C$ FROM q;

-- Q24 [Peroxisomes · hard · Skill 2 · ans A] (g5)
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES (
    'bio_biochem', 'The Cell', 'Peroxisomes',
    $qt5$A patient's cells carry a mutation that blocks proteins bearing peroxisomal targeting sequences from entering peroxisomes, so the peroxisomal matrix enzymes (including catalase) are excluded from the organelle. Which molecule most directly accumulates to damaging levels as a result of this defect?$qt5$,
    $op5$[{"label":"A","text":"Hydrogen peroxide, \\(\\text{H}_2\\text{O}_2\\), because the enzymes that normally break it down inside peroxisomes are absent"},{"label":"B","text":"Oxygen, \\(\\text{O}_2\\), because the enzymes that normally produce it inside peroxisomes are absent"},{"label":"C","text":"Carbon dioxide, \\(\\text{CO}_2\\), because the reactions that normally consume it inside peroxisomes are absent"},{"label":"D","text":"Pyruvate, because the oxidation reactions that normally consume it inside peroxisomes are absent"}]$op5$::jsonb,
    'A',
    $ex5$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because the peroxisomal oxidation reactions release \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), and the catalase that safely breaks it down acts only when confined inside the peroxisome, so excluding that enzyme lets \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) build up to damaging levels. OpenStax Biology 2e states that peroxisomal oxidation reactions "release hydrogen peroxide, \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\), which would be damaging to cells; however, when these reactions are confined to peroxisomes, enzymes safely break down the \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) into oxygen and water" (lines 5510-5511). The import defect keeps catalase out of the peroxisome, so the molecule that the organelle's enzymes would normally destroy is the one that accumulates and harms the cell. (Choice A) Correct: the text identifies \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) as the damaging molecule that intact peroxisomes neutralize with confined enzymes; remove those enzymes and \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) is no longer broken down, so it accumulates (lines 5510-5511). The single-membrane peroxisome relies on importing its matrix enzymes, so a targeting-sequence defect directly leaves \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) undetoxified. (Choice B) Oxygen is a PRODUCT of catalase breaking \(\text{H}_2\text{O}_2\) "into oxygen and water" (line 5511); with catalase absent the breakdown reaction does not run, so this product is made less, not accumulated. This reverses the product-versus-substrate relationship. (Choice C) Carbon dioxide is a waste PRODUCT of mitochondrial cellular respiration, where "this process uses oxygen and produces carbon dioxide as a waste product" (lines 5476-5477); it is not a peroxisomal substrate, so the peroxisomal defect does not cause it to accumulate. (Choice D) Pyruvate is consumed by oxidation reactions in the mitochondria during cellular respiration, the ATP-making organelle that uses oxygen (lines 5473-5476), not in peroxisomes, so a peroxisomal-import defect does not block its consumption. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must chain the loss of peroxisomal enzyme import to the specific molecule the organelle's confined enzymes would otherwise destroy.$ex5$,
    'hard', '2A', $cc5$Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)$cc5$, 'biology', 2, 150
  ) RETURNING id
)
